\begin{abstract}
We compare multinomial logistic regression (LR) with $k$-nearest neighbours
($k$NN, $k$ tuned by inner cross-validation) on Iris (OpenML 61) by 10
repetitions of stratified 5-fold cross-validation on shared splits. LR reaches a mean accuracy of \LRMean\%
(95\% interval \LRLo--\LRHi\%) and $k$NN \KNNMean\% (\KNNLo--\KNNHi\%). The
paired difference LR$-k$NN is \DiffMean{} percentage points (pp), 95\% interval
[\DiffLo, \DiffHi]~pp: the data do not distinguish the methods. Runs on a KAUST
Ibex CPU node (\IbexNode) and locally \RunsIdentical.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
On Fisher's Iris data~\citep{fisher1936} both a linear classifier (multinomial
LR) and a non-parametric one ($k$NN) are accurate, so
the question is whether their difference can be resolved with 150 samples. We
use a paired repeated cross-validation design with an interval that accounts
for overlapping training sets~\citep{nadeau2003inference}, and check exact
reproducibility across two machines.

\section{Data and methods}
\paragraph{Data.} Iris was downloaded by the delivered script from
OpenML~\citep{vanschoren2014openml} (dataset 61, version \OpenMLVersion, ARFF
MD5 \texttt{\DataMDFive}; 150 rows, 3 classes of 50).

\paragraph{Models.} Both are scikit-learn~\citep{pedregosa2011scikit} pipelines
that standardise features on the training fold only. LR is a multinomial
(softmax) model with fixed hyperparameters (\LRSettings); only $k$NN is tuned.
$k$NN (uniform weights, Euclidean) selects $k$ from \KGrid{} on each outer
training fold by stratified \InnerFolds-fold inner cross-validation (ties: smallest $k$).

\paragraph{Protocol and intervals.} The outer loop is 10 repetitions of
stratified 5-fold cross-validation (seed \Seed): \NFolds{} folds of \NTrain{}
training and \NTest{} test points, shared by both methods so that differences
are paired. Each mean over the \NFolds{} folds gets a 95\% corrected resampled
$t$ interval~\citep{nadeau2003inference,bouckaert2004replicability},
$\bar x \pm t_{0.975,49}\sqrt{(1/50+30/120)\,s^2}$ with $s^2$ the variance of
the fold values; the correction accounts for overlapping training sets, which
make the naive $t$ interval too narrow~\citep{dietterich1998approximate}. For contrast
only, we also give naive $t$ and fold-bootstrap intervals, which ignore it.

