LR attains \LRMean\% [\LRLo, \LRHi] and $k$NN \KNNMean\% [\KNNLo, \KNNHi]
(Table~\ref{tab:results}). The paired difference LR$-k$NN is \DiffMean~pp,
corrected 95\% interval [\DiffLo, \DiffHi]~pp ($p=\DiffP$; Figure~\ref{fig:accuracy}).
It contains zero: the methods are not distinguishable on Iris, and differences
of a few points either way cannot be excluded. The naive $t$
[\DiffNaiveLo, \DiffNaiveHi]~pp and bootstrap [\DiffBootLo, \DiffBootHi]~pp
intervals are much narrower (the latter excludes zero) only because they treat
overlapping folds as independent. The selected $k$ ranges over \KRange{}
(median \KMedian). With five other split seeds, without standardisation, or
with LR's $C$ tuned, every corrected difference interval still contains zero
(\texttt{results/sensitivity.json}).

\paragraph{Ibex versus local.} The same script, data and packages (NumPy
\NumpyVersion, SciPy \ScipyVersion, scikit-learn \SklearnVersion; one BLAS
thread) ran as a one-CPU SLURM job on KAUST Ibex (job \IbexJobID, node
\IbexNode{} as recorded in the job, Python \PythonVersionIbex) and locally
(Python \PythonVersion). Per-fold accuracies, selected $k$ and all predictions
are byte-identical (max.\ abs.\ difference \MaxAbsDiff): the runs \RunsIdentical.

\paragraph{Limitations.} With \NTest{} test points per fold accuracies move in
steps of $1/30$; the corrected interval is only approximately calibrated; LR
was not tuned; and one small, nearly separable dataset says nothing about
harder problems. \textbf{Code} for every number, the figure and the table is
next to this PDF in \texttt{code/}, \texttt{results/}, \texttt{figures/} and
\texttt{tables/}.

